    explanation: 'The book marks who is responsible for each claim. Sentences about the Qur’an (“is told in”, “is not described in”, “are referred to as … without”) say what the source contains or leaves out. Impersonal passives (“It is also reported that …”, “It was narrated that …”) and “According to …” pass on a report and keep some distance between the writer and the claim. Verbs of meaning and framing phrases (“It clearly appears that …”, “refer to the fact that …”, “In fact, …”) introduce the writer’s own reading. A careful B2 reader keeps these three levels apart when summarising.',
    feedback: { correct: 'Well done. You separated the source, reported information and the writer’s interpretation.', incorrect: 'Look at the opening words of each sentence: does it describe the Qur’an, pass on a report (reported, narrated, according to), or give the writer’s reading (appears, refer to, in fact)?' },
